    {
      "id": 17,
      "kind": "mother_usage",
      "payload": "{\"model_calls\":1,\"phase\":\"synthesis\",\"plan\":{\"A\":\"Explore a generic four-core parallel merge sort as an alternative to the measured task-based quicksorts: sort disjoint chunks, then merge them in parallel where profitable. Preserve signed integer ordering and the existing generation, validation, and MD5 behavior; handle small inputs and allocation failure safely. Compare full-program correctness and timings against the current best.\",\"B\":\"Investigate a radix-sort alternative for the generated integer array. Use a generic signed-int ordering transform and enough passes for the full integer width, not assumptions about particular generated values. Keep generation and checksum unchanged, and measure whether the extra memory traffic and auxiliary storage pay off on this target.\",\"C\":\"Refine the measured fastest quicksort candidate (0.0270 s): it combines three-way partitioning, insertion sort for small ranges, bounded recursion, independent pivot state, and OpenMP tasks for large arrays. Sweep generic parallel/task and small-range cutoffs one at a time; preserve disjoint task ranges and avoid sharing RNG state. Recheck correctness and end-to-end time after each change.\",\"G\":\"Use the 0.0270 s C candidate as the current measured incumbent; the baseline is 0.0690 s. Compare complete candidate programs under the same correctness and timing procedure, retaining the fastest valid result. Do not assume individually successful changes combine additively: test any integration directly. Preserve input handling, generated values, signed ordering, sortedness validation, and the exact MD5 output; do not alter the harness or build constraints.\",\"analysis\":\"The baseline is valid at 0.0690 s. The best observed candidate is C at 0.0270 s (~2.55× faster); A's best is 0.0355 s and another C candidate is 0.0474 s. The measured is_sorted SIMD changes are effectively neutral or slower (0.0688–0.0701 s), so further effort should target sorting rather than repeat those changes. The ARM64 target has four cores. All candidates must still sort the actual generated data correctly and produce the same checksum; the supplied example or any particular test values are not optimization assumptions.\"},\"request_sha256\":\"7201cdda6e9596bf29a41330b16b8b71d44d97881f8b1e2c2022acc1dacb8fd2\",\"response\":{\"data\":{\"A\":\"Explore a generic four-core parallel merge sort as an alternative to the measured task-based quicksorts: sort disjoint chunks, then merge them in parallel where profitable. Preserve signed integer ordering and the existing generation, validation, and MD5 behavior; handle small inputs and allocation failure safely. Compare full-program correctness and timings against the current best.\",\"B\":\"Investigate a radix-sort alternative for the generated integer array. Use a generic signed-int ordering transform and enough passes for the full integer width, not assumptions about particular generated values. Keep generation and checksum unchanged, and measure whether the extra memory traffic and auxiliary storage pay off on this target.\",\"C\":\"Refine the measured fastest quicksort candidate (0.0270 s): it combines three-way partitioning, insertion sort for small ranges, bounded recursion, independent pivot state, and OpenMP tasks for large arrays. Sweep generic parallel/task and small-range cutoffs one at a time; preserve disjoint task ranges and avoid sharing RNG state. Recheck correctness and end-to-end time after each change.\",\"G\":\"Use the 0.0270 s C candidate as the current measured incumbent; the baseline is 0.0690 s. Compare complete candidate programs under the same correctness and timing procedure, retaining the fastest valid result. Do not assume individually successful changes combine additively: test any integration directly. Preserve input handling, generated values, signed ordering, sortedness validation, and the exact MD5 output; do not alter the harness or build constraints.\",\"analysis\":\"The baseline is valid at 0.0690 s. The best observed candidate is C at 0.0270 s (~2.55× faster); A's best is 0.0355 s and another C candidate is 0.0474 s. The measured is_sorted SIMD changes are effectively neutral or slower (0.0688–0.0701 s), so further effort should target sorting rather than repeat those changes. The ARM64 target has four cores. All candidates must still sort the actual generated data correctly and produce the same checksum; the supplied example or any particular test values are not optimization assumptions.\"},\"error\":null,\"infrastructure_error\":false,\"model\":\"gpt-6-luna\",\"nonce\":\"19bf902d13df419386df412fcad98e32\",\"protocol\":\"hpc-forest-codex-mailbox-v1\",\"reasoning_effort\":\"xhigh\",\"request_sha256\":\"7201cdda6e9596bf29a41330b16b8b71d44d97881f8b1e2c2022acc1dacb8fd2\",\"session_id\":\"fb704484f3a5459ca3a4a358d2208a0b\",\"usage\":{\"cached_tokens_included_in_input\":true,\"codex_jsonl_sha256\":\"a3aa2e5556eaa308d1282844390823d01b19d5fb7763ee9f57cb1a6c82cc0b00\",\"events\":[{\"cache_write_input_tokens\":0,\"cached_input_tokens\":0,\"input_tokens\":23139,\"output_tokens\":4069,\"reasoning_output_tokens\":3624}],\"llm_calls\":1,\"model_requested\":\"gpt-6-luna\",\"reasoning_effort_requested\":\"xhigh\",\"token_accounting\":\"exact\",\"tokens\":27208,\"tool_events\":[]}},\"status\":\"complete\"}",
